WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
[Music begins] [Michael F. Chiang, MD]&nbsp;
I'm an ophthalmologist by background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:07.280
and I actually started my career in&nbsp;
2001 at Columbia University as a NLM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.080
postdoctoral fellow. And since then,&nbsp;
I've spent 20 years in academia as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.240
clinician scientist working at the interface&nbsp;
of ophthalmology and biomedical informatics.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.800
In November 2020, I started a new job at NIH&nbsp;
as director of the National Eye Institute.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.600
And I'm really excited to be a investigator&nbsp;
here at the National Library of Medicine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.480
because you know in a way it's&nbsp;
what I started my career doing.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.560
At the National Eye Institute, our&nbsp;
mission is to eliminate vision loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.520
and improve quality of life through vision&nbsp;
research, and that's an application domain. And

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.000
at the National Library of Medicine, what we&nbsp;
do is methodological research in biomedical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.200
informatics and data science.&nbsp;
And I think it's really important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.680
to connect the methodology with the application.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.560
One of the things that I do is I&nbsp;
develop artificial intelligence methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.200
to diagnose a pediatric retinal disease&nbsp;
called retinopathy of prematurity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.320
It's one of the leading causes of&nbsp;
blindness in babies around the world.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.600
Stevie Wonder is someone who went blind from ROP&nbsp;
in the 1950s before there was a treatment for it.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.880
And we've developed an AI system that received FDA&nbsp;
breakthrough status. And one of the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.880
we've been doing is trying to find ways to apply&nbsp;
AI to real world problems in eye care for kids.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.600
Babies who are premature, and therefore&nbsp;
at risk for retinopathy of prematurity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.560
come disproportionately from urban and rural&nbsp;
medically underserved areas. And there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.200
huge challenges in terms of delivering eye&nbsp;
care and health care overall to those babies.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.880
And so, one of the things that I did at&nbsp;
the very beginning of my career is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.480
we work to develop and validate telemedicine&nbsp;
systems for retinopathy prematurity diagnosis.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.640
And now what we're starting to do is to&nbsp;
experiment with - can we implement AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.120
artificial intelligence, into those&nbsp;
telemedicine systems to be able to improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.280
the accuracy and the quality of the&nbsp;
diagnosis that we're able to make remotely.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.360
There's never been a time in history&nbsp;
where we've had so much access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.960
to science and technology as&nbsp;
being applied to healthcare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.280
And I think one of the challenges for the&nbsp;
future is - how to move things like biomedical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.101
informatics and artificial intelligence and&nbsp;
data science into the mainstream of healthcare.

00:02:12.881 --> 00:02:14.063
[Music]

00:02:14.063 --> 00:02:16.873
[Music fades to silence]

